 proud of  and like sort of becomes you know and it  like i said it's like  you know like you can sort of measure it  in how much merchandise  well and you mentioned um you know not  not knowing how you did it and the  making of doc i've seen a number of  times that every time i watch it i just  feel exhausted for you  just watching how you guys made that um  but i'm a huge fan of bill pope and i  wanted to ask you  um you know i i read that you know part  of hiring was  to kind of calm any concerns on  universal's part  of you handling a budget that big but i  was just curious how that relationship  started because  i mean you guys have worked together a  number of times since then and  you know how that collaboration went  forward and then what his reaction was  when you were like  this is what we're gonna do these are  how many different shots and setups  we're doing here  well it's interesting you mentioned that  because it was something that  you know uh and it's interesting because  jess hall who i've worked with on hot  fuzz who did an amazing job on that film  and is an incredible  talent like because we were both kind of  sort of up and comers with that film  it wasn't something where like because  universal were nervous about it  initially with bill initially it was  like a slightly  strategic thing is that like okay so  they're nervous about  what this is but they'll be less nervous  if i hire  the cinematographer of the matrix and  then you know and then it's and that is  exactly  initially the reason that i met with  bill is because then your universe will  be like oh  he's got the matrix guy okay this makes  sense now i get it which is sort of how  that works  however the thing with bill which was  the surprise  is that bill in terms of like  pitch in to answer your question  pitching it to him about this is what  we're going to do  bill has sort of done every shot that  has ever  existed so he is in the most brilliant  way kind of unflappable if he has the  kind of the time and the tools  there's nothing really that phases him  but that wasn't the thing is  that wasn't the thing that i really  loved about working with him and  still love working with him you know  it's because he really  for somebody who's like such a brilliant  visual artist  his main concern is about  the emotion and the characters so when i  met him  to talk about the scott pilgrim script  we only talked about  the emotional aspect of it and about the  characters and that side of it  so i came away from that meeting  thinking well that's unexpected  it was almost like he kind of like saw  was completely unfazed by my visual  ambitions  it was more focused on you know kind of  what was at the heart of the story  and and really that's something that i  he's so brilliant with actors bill  and he's such a brilliant um  he sort of really shoots actors in an  incredibly  kind of um what's the word um  but you know kind of like i say intimate  but it's  something that like he he gets the best  out of them  visually in terms of just kind of like  or  capturing what they do but making sure  they look amazing doing it  and i think you can see that you know  especially in like scott pilgrim and  baby driver  i i skipped over the world zen because  everybody's supposed to look terrible in  that baby  and that's uh maybe my favorite of your  films i i thanked  that film i think simon's incredible in  that movie he is i think that's simon's  best  performance i think he would agree with  that as well a big topic of  conversation among the fans is obviously  the ending and and you know the  the original ending is on the blu-ray  and i was always just curious from your  perspective  um i know that didn't test as well you  shot a new ending  what do you have a preference between  the two endings do you think  that one works no i think the theatrical  one is the one i think the thing is  is that we wrote that ending  and i think when we me and michael  because